{"module":"lgbtq","voice":"female","duration":157.0,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Black LGBTQ history","start":0.0,"end":2.29,"words":[[0.04,0.4],[0.4,1.49],[1.49,2.13]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Some of the movement's most important strategists were gay, lesbian or gender-nonconforming, and many were pushed to the side because of it.","start":3.09,"end":11.06,"words":[[3.14,3.34],[3.34,3.47],[3.47,3.61],[3.61,4.15],[4.15,4.48],[4.48,5.02],[5.02,5.83],[5.83,5.96],[5.96,6.23],[6.47,6.94],[6.94,7.01],[7.01,8.22],[8.46,8.66],[8.66,8.93],[8.93,9.06],[9.06,9.33],[9.33,9.47],[9.47,9.6],[9.6,9.87],[9.87,10.21],[10.21,10.34],[10.34,10.54]]},{"el":"intro","text":"San Francisco's CAREN Act protects sexual orientation and gender identity too.","start":11.41,"end":16.61,"words":[[11.43,11.64],[11.64,12.39],[12.39,12.73],[12.73,12.94],[12.94,13.48],[13.48,13.96],[13.96,14.71],[14.71,14.92],[14.92,15.33],[15.33,15.94],[15.94,16.15]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Bayard Rustin","start":16.96,"end":18.2,"words":[[17.02,17.51],[17.51,18.08]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Rustin taught Dr. King about Gandhian nonviolence and was the chief organizer of the 1963 March on Washington.","start":18.9,"end":26.98,"words":[[18.95,19.39],[19.39,19.61],[19.61,20.05],[20.42,20.64],[20.64,21.01],[21.01,21.6],[21.6,22.41],[22.41,22.63],[22.63,22.85],[22.85,23.0],[23.0,23.3],[23.3,23.89],[23.89,24.03],[24.03,24.18],[24.18,25.36],[25.36,25.66],[25.66,25.8],[25.8,26.47]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Because he was openly gay, and had been arrested in 1953 on a “morals” charge, other leaders kept him out of the spotlight; Senator Strom Thurmond attacked him on the Senate floor weeks before the march.","start":27.33,"end":40.35,"words":[[27.39,27.77],[27.77,27.92],[27.92,28.15],[28.15,28.68],[28.68,28.99],[29.25,29.48],[29.48,29.71],[29.71,29.94],[29.94,30.47],[30.47,30.62],[30.62,31.77],[31.77,31.92],[31.92,32.07],[32.07,32.53],[32.53,32.99],[33.25,33.48],[33.48,33.86],[33.86,34.17],[34.17,34.4],[34.4,34.63],[34.63,34.78],[34.78,34.93],[34.93,35.62],[35.88,36.34],[36.34,36.72],[36.72,37.18],[37.18,37.56],[37.56,37.79],[37.79,37.94],[37.94,38.09],[38.09,38.47],[38.47,38.7],[38.7,39.01],[39.01,39.31],[39.31,39.46],[39.46,39.85]]},{"el":"s0","text":"He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom posthumously in 2013, and California pardoned his 1953 conviction in 2020.","start":40.7,"end":50.41,"words":[[40.73,40.88],[40.88,41.32],[41.32,41.47],[41.47,42.28],[42.28,42.65],[42.65,42.8],[42.8,43.24],[43.24,44.2],[44.2,44.35],[44.35,45.24],[45.5,45.72],[45.72,46.38],[46.38,46.83],[46.83,47.05],[47.05,48.16],[48.16,48.82],[48.82,48.97],[48.97,49.93]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Harlem and literature","start":51.31,"end":52.92,"words":[[51.35,51.79],[51.79,52.06],[52.06,52.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Many Harlem Renaissance figures, including philosopher Alain Locke and performer Gladys Bentley, lived openly or semi-openly as gay or lesbian.","start":53.62,"end":63.58,"words":[[53.68,54.01],[54.01,54.42],[54.42,55.15],[55.15,55.64],[55.92,56.58],[56.58,57.23],[57.23,57.64],[57.64,57.88],[57.88,58.13],[58.13,58.62],[58.62,59.11],[59.11,59.68],[59.96,60.29],[60.29,60.86],[60.86,60.94],[60.94,61.92],[61.92,62.08],[62.08,62.33],[62.33,62.41],[62.41,63.06]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Whether others, such as Langston Hughes, were gay is debated by biographers.","start":63.93,"end":69.44,"words":[[63.98,64.3],[64.3,64.69],[64.96,65.27],[65.27,65.43],[65.43,65.97],[65.97,66.36],[66.64,66.79],[66.79,67.03],[67.03,67.18],[67.18,67.81],[67.81,68.04],[68.04,68.9]]},{"el":"s1","text":"James Baldwin wrote openly about gay love in Giovanni's Room (1956), and poet Audre Lorde described herself as a “Black, lesbian, mother, warrior, poet.”","start":69.79,"end":81.11,"words":[[69.85,70.29],[70.29,70.8],[70.8,71.09],[71.09,71.6],[71.6,71.97],[71.97,72.19],[72.19,72.41],[72.41,72.55],[72.55,73.14],[73.14,73.36],[73.36,74.6],[74.86,75.08],[75.08,75.44],[75.44,75.66],[75.66,75.88],[75.88,76.54],[76.54,76.98],[76.98,77.13],[77.13,77.27],[77.27,77.64],[77.89,78.48],[78.74,79.1],[79.36,79.87],[80.13,80.57]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Pauli Murray","start":82.01,"end":83.1,"words":[[82.05,82.45],[82.45,82.94]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Pauli Murray's legal writing argued that segregation violated the 14th Amendment, an argument Thurgood Marshall's team drew on for Brown, and later shaped Ruth Bader Ginsburg's sex-discrimination cases.","start":83.8,"end":96.84,"words":[[83.87,84.16],[84.16,84.61],[84.61,84.98],[84.98,85.43],[85.43,85.8],[85.8,86.02],[86.02,86.84],[86.84,87.58],[87.58,87.73],[87.73,88.17],[88.17,88.92],[89.18,89.32],[89.32,89.92],[89.92,90.29],[90.29,90.74],[90.74,90.96],[90.96,91.18],[91.18,91.33],[91.33,91.48],[91.48,91.92],[92.18,92.41],[92.41,92.78],[92.78,93.15],[93.15,93.37],[93.37,93.74],[93.74,94.41],[94.41,95.75],[95.75,96.27]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Murray co-founded the National Organization for Women and in 1977 became the first Black woman ordained an Episcopal priest.","start":97.19,"end":106.25,"words":[[97.24,97.62],[97.62,98.39],[98.39,98.54],[98.54,99.08],[99.08,100.0],[100.0,100.15],[100.15,100.54],[100.54,100.77],[100.77,100.92],[100.92,102.38],[102.38,102.84],[102.84,103.0],[103.0,103.3],[103.3,103.61],[103.61,103.99],[103.99,104.46],[104.46,104.61],[104.61,105.3],[105.3,105.76]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Murray's private papers describe a lifelong struggle with gender identity.","start":106.6,"end":111.48,"words":[[106.65,107.07],[107.07,107.56],[107.56,107.98],[107.98,108.54],[108.54,108.68],[108.68,109.17],[109.17,109.66],[109.66,109.87],[109.87,110.29],[110.29,110.99]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Stonewall","start":112.38,"end":113.66,"words":[[112.42,113.49]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The 1969 Stonewall uprising in New York is often credited to Marsha P. Johnson, a Black trans activist, and Sylvia Rivera.","start":114.36,"end":124.77,"words":[[114.41,114.56],[114.56,115.92],[115.92,116.55],[116.55,117.19],[117.19,117.35],[117.35,117.59],[117.59,117.82],[117.82,117.98],[117.98,118.3],[118.3,118.94],[118.94,119.1],[119.1,119.42],[119.42,119.65],[120.05,120.69],[120.97,121.12],[121.12,121.44],[121.44,121.84],[121.84,122.56],[122.83,123.07],[123.07,123.63],[123.63,124.27]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Johnson herself said she arrived after the fighting began; witnesses also credit Stormé DeLarverie, a Black butch lesbian.","start":125.12,"end":132.91,"words":[[125.16,125.67],[125.67,126.1],[126.1,126.32],[126.32,126.46],[126.46,126.9],[126.9,127.18],[127.18,127.33],[127.33,127.76],[127.76,128.2],[128.45,129.03],[129.03,129.39],[129.39,129.82],[129.82,130.26],[130.26,130.91],[131.16,131.3],[131.3,131.59],[131.59,131.88],[131.88,132.46]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Their activism afterward, including founding a shelter for homeless trans youth, is documented.","start":133.26,"end":139.9,"words":[[133.33,133.56],[133.56,134.23],[134.23,134.83],[135.09,135.69],[135.69,136.22],[136.22,136.37],[136.37,136.74],[136.74,136.89],[136.89,137.41],[137.41,137.79],[137.79,138.09],[138.35,138.5],[138.5,139.4]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":140.8,"end":141.63,"words":[[140.85,141.47]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Human Rights Campaign tracking of fatal violence against transgender people in the U.S. finds that a majority of the victims are Black trans women.","start":142.33,"end":150.94,"words":[[142.36,142.75],[142.75,143.08],[143.08,143.55],[143.55,143.94],[143.94,144.07],[144.07,144.47],[144.47,145.0],[145.0,145.4],[145.4,146.12],[146.12,146.45],[146.45,146.59],[146.59,146.72],[146.72,147.05],[147.38,147.78],[147.78,147.97],[147.97,148.11],[148.11,148.7],[148.7,148.83],[148.83,148.97],[148.97,149.43],[149.43,149.49],[149.49,149.76],[149.76,150.09],[150.09,150.49]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Calls to police about people's gender or sexuality are a form of the harm the CAREN Act targets.","start":151.29,"end":156.09,"words":[[151.31,151.56],[151.56,151.69],[151.69,152.0],[152.0,152.32],[152.32,152.7],[152.7,153.07],[153.07,153.14],[153.14,153.77],[153.77,153.83],[153.83,153.95],[153.95,154.14],[154.14,154.27],[154.27,154.4],[154.4,154.58],[154.58,154.71],[154.71,155.03],[155.03,155.21],[155.21,155.65]]}]}